    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d625">

                <pb facs="0748" n="646" sameAs="frus:frus1919v01/pg646"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.003/451</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Reinsch</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi><anchor n="69"
                        xml:id="d625-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1917-12-19"><hi rendition="#italic">December 19,
                            1917</hi>.</date><lb/>[<hi rendition="#italic">Received January 16,
                            1918.</hi>]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1811</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to point out that there
                    seems to have been a misunderstanding in the reading of my telegram of November
                    19, 7 p.m.,<anchor n="70" xml:id="d625-f2"/> relating to the special customs
                    duty arrangement on the Manchurian frontier. Your telegram of December 16th [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">15th</hi>], 6 p.m.,<anchor n="71" xml:id="d625-f3"/>
                    states that it is inexact that the French Government has <pb facs="0749" n="647"
                        xml:id="pg647"/> taken the initiative in asking the exemption, etc. My
                    telegram of November 19, 7 p.m. stated merely that the British Minister believed
                    that his government was urging the French Government to relinquish the precedent
                    which is the basis of the Russian claim. This precedent is the arrangement which
                    was made by the Convention of June 26, 1887, between France and China, which
                    diminishes by 3/10 the tariff on goods transported through the treaty ports of
                    the Franco-Chinese border, together with the understanding, in 1902, that the
                    customs duties paid on the Franco-Chinese frontier should not be affected by the
                    revision then made. It is this latter precedent which it is believed the Russian
                    Legation was relying on in its present demand that the new revision should not
                    affect the duties as actually levied at present on the Russo-Manchurian
                    frontier. I also understand that while the British Government had made similar
                    arrangements with respect to the Burma-Chinese frontier, it is ready to forego
                    the privilege of having the duties there remain unchanged and unaffected by the
                    impending revision.</p>
                <p>I have the honor to refer to my despatch No. 1715, of November 10, 1917.<anchor
                        n="72" xml:id="d625-f4"/></p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Paul S. Reinsch</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
